<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Paphos</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 BCE – 306 BCE (approx.) · capital Paphos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient Cypriot kingdom centred on the great sanctuary of Aphrodite, whose kings served as the goddess's high priests for some eight centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Paphos grew at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaepaphos">Palaepaphos</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouklia">Kouklia</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> town in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. In the 12th and 11th centuries BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> settlers arrived, and Greek legend credited Agapenor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tegea">Tegea</a> with founding the city after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. An inscription of the 11th century BCE shows Greek already written there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings combined royal power with the priesthood of the Paphian goddess, later identified with Aphrodite, whose open-air sanctuary with a conical stone drew pilgrims from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Paphos used the Cypriot syllabary for Greek and struck its own coins. It paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and later to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and joined the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 498 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicocles-of-paphos">Nicocles</a>, founded the harbour city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nea-paphos">Nea Paphos</a>, which became the capital of Ptolemaic and Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. The kingdom was abolished when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> took the island, by c. 306 BCE, but the sanctuary of Aphrodite flourished into late antiquity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1100 BCE – 306 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:37.81%;width:15.80%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 BCE – 306 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Paphos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicocles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cult of the Paphian Aphrodite</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arcado-Cypriot Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Paphos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-703&amp;focus=kingdom-of-paphos" aria-label="Kingdom of Paphos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5503 919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Paphos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5823 1175L6092 1134M5823 1175L6140 1251M5823 1175L5772 1297"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5823 1175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5823 1175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Paphos. <a href="/atlas?year=-703&amp;focus=kingdom-of-paphos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

